    <!-- Onboarding guide: the exact commands -->
    <string name="onboarding_guide_title">Getting your server onto the network</string>
    <string name="onboarding_step_lan">1. Let the server listen on your network</string>
    <string name="onboarding_step_lan_commands">opencode service set hostname 0.0.0.0\nopencode service start</string>
    <string name="onboarding_step_lan_desc">The server listens on localhost only until this is set. Then run opencode pair to get a link and a QR code.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_step_pair">2. Get a one-time pairing link</string>
    <string name="onboarding_step_pair_command">opencode pair</string>
    <string name="onboarding_step_pair_desc">The link works once and expires after five minutes. Scanning it stores a 30-day token on this phone.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_step_serve">Alternative: run the server in the foreground</string>
    <string name="onboarding_step_serve_command">opencode serve --hostname 0.0.0.0</string>
    <string name="onboarding_step_serve_desc">Use this instead of the service commands when you want the server to stop with the terminal.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_step_ssh">3. Keep it on localhost with an SSH tunnel</string>
    <string name="onboarding_step_ssh_command">ssh -N -L 4096:localhost:4096 user@computer</string>
    <string name="onboarding_step_ssh_desc">Run the tunnel, then enter http://localhost:4096 here. Nothing is exposed to the network.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_step_tailscale">4. Reach it over Tailscale</string>
    <string name="onboarding_step_tailscale_desc">With Tailscale on both devices, use the computer\'s Tailscale address or MagicDNS name, for example http://my-computer:4096.</string>
    <string name="onboarding_https_note">Traffic on a local network is plain HTTP. Use HTTPS, an SSH tunnel or Tailscale for anything you would not send in the clear.</string>

